1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,670
Who is directing whom?

2
00:00:07,310 --> 00:00:14,370
A voice gives an instruction. Two performers
test it. A phone enters the frame, and a second
camera watches the first.

3
00:00:14,970 --> 00:00:18,750
Before anyone can answer, the rehearsal has
already changed authors.

4
00:00:19,370 --> 00:00:22,310
This is not evidence of a private couple
breaking up.

5
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:28,161
The breakup is a proposition—material placed in
public so that everyone in the room can push
back.

6
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,770
3P begins with Performing, but performance here
is not the polished result.

7
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:04,950
It is the moment a suggestion acquires a body.

8
00:01:05,430 --> 00:01:10,750
A line becomes a posture; a posture provokes a
correction; the correction becomes the next
event.

9
00:01:11,310 --> 00:01:14,250
The performers do not simply deliver a
director's idea.

10
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:20,822
Their timing, discomfort, refusal, and invention
make decisions the instruction could never make
on its own.

11
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,210
The second P is Peering: looking closely, and
being caught in the act of looking.

12
00:02:00,690 --> 00:02:09,030
One image holds several positions at once—the
performer, the witness, the phone, the fixed
camera, and the person deciding where to stand.

13
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:11,410
None of them sees the whole rehearsal.

14
00:02:11,990 --> 00:02:15,930
Each angle selects a different event, so
observation is never neutral.

15
00:02:16,330 --> 00:02:18,818
To peer is already to edit the room.

16
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,090
The third P is Processing.

17
00:02:57,550 --> 00:03:03,330
A gesture is performed, watched, recorded,
replayed, discussed, and returned to the bodies
that made it.

18
00:03:03,770 --> 00:03:06,090
The camera does not wait outside this cycle.

19
00:03:07,310 --> 00:03:09,330
Its distance, delay, and framing become new
instructions.

20
00:03:10,370 --> 00:03:14,813
The rehearsal thinks by processing what has just
happened, before anyone can stabilize a single
meaning.

21
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,290
A rehearsal becomes interesting precisely when
the scene stops obeying.

22
00:03:49,410 --> 00:03:55,770
An instruction lands badly. A body hesitates.
Someone laughs. Another person interferes.

23
00:03:56,350 --> 00:04:00,390
These are not errors to remove from the final
work; they are information about the work.

24
00:04:01,230 --> 00:04:03,690
Refusal reveals the hidden demand inside a
direction.

25
00:04:04,210 --> 00:04:08,073
Confusion shows that the supposed author and the
actual event are no longer the same thing.

26
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:49,250
The title director suggests a stable center, but
direction keeps moving.

27
00:04:49,570 --> 00:05:00,090
It passes from the person who proposes the
breakup, to the performer who changes its
temperature, to the witness who reframes it, and
to the camera that preserves one version while
losing another.

28
00:05:00,710 --> 00:05:03,410
Credit may remain singular; creation does not.

29
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:06,830
The scene is directed by a temporary
distribution of attention.

30
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:45,870
What, then, is being rehearsed?

31
00:05:46,330 --> 00:05:50,450
Not a private romance, and not a perfect scene
waiting to be discovered.

32
00:05:51,030 --> 00:05:58,950
What is rehearsed is a way of thinking in
public: acting before certainty, watching
without possessing, and changing course when the
room answers back.

33
00:05:59,390 --> 00:06:03,430
3P—Performing, Peering, Processing—names that
circulation.

34
00:06:04,370 --> 00:06:10,960
The question is not whether the director
disappears. It is whether direction can remain
answerable to everyone who makes the event
possible.

